Anybody else have issues like this?

My usual NetHack game lasts ten minutes to an hour
or so, then YASD, YASD, YASD.

When I get a good game going that lasts for days, it
gets to the point I'm almost afraid to play, knowing
there is _still_ a YASD somewhere just out of sight
ahead.

My current healer, at AC -17 and XL 11 is a case in
point.

Little glories like a co-aligned Mines Town altar,
permitting turning 25 potions at once into holy
water, have really made the game look promising.

My PC now knows lots more spells than can be cast in
his current armor, but is missing some of the really
crucial ones, like ID.

So, there is a box near an altar just full of unIDed
scrolls, and the unIDed potions seemed more useful
as holy water, thus the pool swimming exercises.

Even Giantslayer, enchanted up, makes a pretty good
weapon, and my deity decided I should start my PC
with something not too powerful in the way of an
artifact weapon, I guess, so he doesn't hurt
himself.

Without IDing, though, it wasn't possible safely to
apply all the ?oEW to that one toothpick, so, with
the blessing that my PC went forgetful on "healing",
the last one went to rustproofing a kit of daggars,
sadly a bit too late for nine of them.

Have I mentioned how much I _hate_ rust monsters?

This is pretty much a second full kit of armor after
one rusted to ruin in Sokoban, and on a PC who
didn't get to do the protection racket at XL 1,
because his stupid pets kept wandering just out of
sight and then dying to unknown causes, leaving him
to fight his own monsters, thank-yoy-very-much, so
with a naked AC of 5 or 4 at best.

[I've taken to naming my pets by the turn at which
they are acquired, I burn through so many of them
some games. This, if I ever start Hearsing, is why
you might find a pet named "Barks A Lot at Oracles
on T20351" in a bones file from me someday. That's
xanthianic for: a mutt that volunteered to join the
forces of light on the Oracle level far longer into
the game than most of my PCs manage to survive.]

But the better my PC gets, the harder it is for me
to start up the game from the save file, just
_knowing_I'm going to do something the moral
equivalent of starving on Sokoban, the second I
touch the keyboard, and then have to kick myself
from one end of the house to the other in response.

This isn't just paranoia, I'm _sure_ this is true,
on solid evidence.

For example, I sent him to take his pick-axe in for
the swim along with the potions (to the quick
emitting of a mighty cat-disturbing "aaaaargh" upon
realizing that), and so had to find a non-very-rusty
replacement pick-axe. Luckily, as slowly as I go
deeper when I play, Mines was littered with the
silly things.

It isn't a matter of _whether_ some stupid mistake
will be made on a regular basis, it's just a matter
of what the _lethal_ error will be.

Where, oh where, do NetHack players go to regain
self-confidence?

xanthian, the suddenly timid.

YANI: confidence pills, dispensed out the side of
the computer monitor when the operating system
detects that NetHack hasn't been started up for a
while.



